movements of limbs. At the attack of muscle cramping, his upper limbs
were fixed in flexion posture and lower limbs in extension posture, His
trunk was as stiff as wood and his face was grimaced under the
unbearable pain. Laboratory tests revealed no abnormal findings in sugar
tolerance, etc., but anti-DNA antibody, antinuclear antibody, and anti-
smooth-muscle antibody were positive. Immunocytochemical study of
antineuronal autoantibodies, including antibodies against GAD and
pancreatic islet cells, was negative (Solimena, Folli ef al., 1988, 1990).
Antimitochondrial antibody, thyroglobulin antibody, thyroid.
antibody, and anti-scleroderma-70 antibody were all negative in the
serum, No oligoclonal bands of IgG were detected in the cerebrospinal
fluid. Chest computed tomography and abdominal ultrasonography
showed no malignancy. Brain MRI revealed only the patient's old left
cerebral infarction. Cervical and lumbar MRI were normal, Surface
EMG revealed continuous muscle activities on muscle cramping which
disappeared five minutes after intravenous injection of diazepam (total
30 mg). Needle EMG showed no myokymia, myotonic discharges, or
other neuropathic changes, and nerve conduction velocity was found to
be normal. The latency of F wave and Renshaw's recurrent inhibition by
H reflex (Bussel and Pirerrot-Deseilligny, 1977) were normal.
Our patient met the criteria for stiff-man syndrome established
by Gordon, Januszko ef al. (1967) and Lorish, Thorsteinsson et al.
(1989), i.e, muscular stiffness and painful muscle cramps which
disappeared with sleep or diazepam injection, and other character
symptoms. Double filtration plasma exchange was performed with PVA
(Plasmacure, Kuraray, Japan) as the first filter and Evaflux 2A (Kuraray,
Japan) as the second. Plasma exchange was carried out four times on an
alternate-day schedule. The total amount of plasma processed was about
12 liters. The patient's medications were not changed during the
treatment. Informed consent concerning the treatment was obtained from
the patient beforehand. Muscular stiffness improved rapidly and painful
muscle cramps disappeared after completing the first session of plasma
exchange (Figure.1). When the administration of diazepam was stopped
after the fourth session of plasma exchange, the improvement of his
symptoms continued and his limb movements and gait returned to a
smooth, normal state. Oral administration of diazepam was not necessary
about six months after plasma exchange. Then, he began to experience
muscle cramps once or twice a week, and oral administration of
